# oe-topics.env — the ONE definition of what gets archived, and of which job archives it.
#
# Sourced by oe-archive-kafka.sh (topic defaults), oe-archive-daily.sh (nightly wrapper),
# oe-archive-dev.sh (2-hourly dev capture) and oe-archive-verify.sh (completeness check).
# Previously each carried its own copy of the same names: equal today, free to drift tomorrow —
# and two environments archiving different topic sets do not produce a comparison, they produce two
# unrelated datasets that merely look comparable. Codex flagged exactly that on 2026-08-08:
# "current equality, not structural prevention".
#
# Change a set HERE and every caller changes with it. Adding a topic is safe (the archiver skips
# topics that do not exist); removing one silently ends its capture, so remove deliberately.
# context-tape.direction.ledger joins this set (A5, 2026-09-08): it is the ONLY dev path that
# archives the calibration corpus. Both dev archive invocations pass TOPICS="$DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE",
# so a ledger absent from this list is a dev corpus that is never captured at all — the dev target
# declaration and the gateway relay would have had nothing behind them (r12 #4).
DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE="dealer-ledger-signal-fired dealer-ledger-outcome-scored dealer-ledger-state dealer-ledger-profile dealer-ledger-calibration-state context-tape.direction.ledger"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JOB SPLIT — why these two sets are DISJOINT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-12: the daily-heavy archive produced nothing for env=prod on 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11
# while the 10-minute spot archive stayed current. Both called oe-archive-kafka.sh with ENV=prod and
# the same ARCHIVE_DIR, so both computed the SAME lock path (/tmp/oe-archive-kafka.prod.<hash>.lock).
# The 10-minute job held it at 17:10; the daily job logged "exiting clean" and returned rc=0.
# Nothing alerted, because exit 0 was being read as proof of archival.
#
# The lock itself was right: two writers into one <topic>.offsets checkpoint file can each advance
# past a range the other never wrote, and that is a GAP. The defect was that the lock's SCOPE was
# the whole environment when its real subject is a TOPIC.
#
# Fixed in two independent layers, either of which alone would have prevented this:
#   1. POLICY (here): the two jobs own disjoint topic sets, so they have nothing to serialise on.
#   2. MECHANISM (oe-archive-kafka.sh): locks are per job AND per topic, so an ad-hoc run with an
#      overlapping list still cannot corrupt a manifest — and cannot starve a scheduled job either.
# Keep these sets disjoint. If they must overlap, layer 2 keeps it correct, but the overlapping
# topic gets skipped by whichever job arrives second and only layer 1 guarantees promptness.

# SPOT — owned by the */10 cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=spot).
# These must be archived far more often than once a day: underlying.spx.price is
# cleanup.policy=compact,delete with min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.01 and segment.ms=1h, so a closed
# segment is compacted almost at once — on 2026-08-03 only 3,306 of 804,629 offsets were still
# readable. Only the ACTIVE segment is safe from the cleaner.
OE_SPOT_TOPICS="underlying.spx.price underlying.spx.index.price underlying.es.price spx.basis.state"

# RECONCILED FROM THE HOST 2026-09-09. This list was widened on .252 on 2026-08-23 and the change was
# never brought back into git, so the repo still held the pre-widening set: deploying it would have cut
# the prod nightly archive from 184 topics to about 17, silently. The drift check refused, which is the
# only reason anyone found out. The host's list is authoritative here and is taken verbatim, plus the
# A5 ledger.
# HEAVY — owned by the 17:10 ET daily wrapper (ARCHIVE_JOB=daily). Multi-million-record topics
# that are written once per session and are pointless to poll every 10 minutes.
#
# 2026-08-12 additions: delta-flow-by-strike and option-price-behavior-by-strike. These are the two
# primary inputs to near-strike bias (NEAR-STRIKE-BIAS-GATE1.md) and they were archived NOWHERE, in
# any environment, against a 1-day Kafka retention — every session of both was being destroyed
# nightly. Both are plain JSON on 32 partitions and carry their own tradingDate.
# 2026-08-23: set widened from 11 topics to 183 — USER: "archive all the topic except
# changelog topic and offset topic". Excluded on purpose (139 topics): Kafka internals
# (__consumer_offsets, __transaction_state, _schemas) and Kafka Streams internals —
# *-changelog, *-rekey, *-repartition. Streams internals are derivable from their source
# topics and are NOT evidence; a changelog also has to stay cleanup.policy=compact, because
# a state store is restored by replaying it whole and a time-truncated changelog restores a
# SILENTLY incomplete store. Adding a topic here is safe (the archiver skips absent ones).
# OWNER RULE 2026-09-17 ("we should only take backup of ibkr, spot and databento raw topic, that's it; rest
# should be excluded"): the nightly heavy pass archives the RAW FEED INPUTS only. Everything downstream is
# derived from these and rebuildable by replay; archiving it filled the NAS (~5 GB/day, 123 GB by 09-17,
# deleted that day). Spot (underlying.spx.price / spx.index.price / es.price / spx.basis.state) is owned by
# the */10 spot cron, not this list. The previous list lives in git history (before this change).
# OWNER RULING 2026-09-19 (gamma-ladder-path requirement §9): the per-strike gamma ladder history is the ONE
# derived topic archived again — the ladder labeller needs historical per-strike dealer-signed gamma and the
# 09-17 deletion took every past day of it. ~150k records/day, far below the raw topics.
# NARROW EXCEPTION 2026-09-20 (VOL-PREMIUM-BASELINE-HISTORY-COVERAGE.md, §5.4 item 6): the vol-premium
# SHADOW engine's own observation ledger options.spx.vol-premium.ivrv, and the version ledger that names which
# baseline was in force, options.spx.vol-premium.baseline. Both are DERIVED, so both are exceptions to the
# 09-17 raw-only rule above, and the exception is made deliberately and ONLY for these two, for the one reason
# replaying the raw feed cannot answer: the §40.8 change distribution is built from the records the engine
# ITSELF published (calibration/IvRvArchiveLoader; §6.4, "a re-derived series would be a second quantity"), so
# it is the single calibration input no replay can reconstruct. Its clock — MIN_CHANGE_SESSIONS=30,
# MIN_CHANGE_SAMPLES=100 — cannot start until those observations are durable OFF the broker, and the coverage
# report found .ivrv in no archived set at all: the shadow deployment was accumulating a record that no
# nightly pass was taking, one broker wipe away from being worth nothing.
#
# MEASURED on the prod broker, session 2026-09-18, read-only (kafka-get-offsets + one assign-mode console read
# that joins no consumer group): offsets 152,849..159,089 = 6,240 offsets, of which 4,428 are COMMITTED RECORDS
# (the remainder are transaction markers), 26,281,817 B raw -> 500,203 B GZIPPED. So ~0.5 MB of NAS per session,
# ~2.5 MB/week, ~0.13 GB/year — against ~5 GB/day for the derived backlog the 09-17 rule deleted, and against
# ~257 MB for two days of options.databento.raw. .baseline holds 0 records today and gains one only when a
# person runs VolPremiumLedgerPublisher --confirm, so its cost is a manifest line a day.
#
# CAPTURE — nothing else needs changing. Both topics are ALREADY in OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS
# (oe-archive-kafka.sh): vol-premium-service refuses to start without exactly_once_v2, so every record is
# written inside a transaction and the console consumer would file ABORTED records as observations the engine
# never published. Naming them here is therefore the whole change — the committed reader, the proven Offset
# layout (they are correctly NOT in OE_COMMITTED_READ_AS_WRITTEN_TOPICS, which is what the calibration loaders
# admit on) and the stable-boundary checkpoint all apply from the first capture, with no legacy checkpoint to
# recapture over. Because there IS no checkpoint yet, that first run reads from offset 0 and files the WHOLE
# retained history in one go — 159,089 offsets, ~138 MB on the broker, ~2.7 MB gzipped, every session since the
# shadow deployment began — under ITS dt=. That is the storage-date rule stated further down, not a defect:
# CommittedLedgerArchive selects a session by the records' own sessionDate, never by the folder name.
#
# options.spx.vol-premium.calendar is DELIBERATELY ABSENT and must stay absent. Owner decision 2026-09-13: the
# calendar ledger is the Postgres table vol_premium_calendar_ledger, not a topic (scripts/kafka/topics.env
# declares it in no list, and no topic of that name exists on the prod broker). Naming it here would archive
# nothing for ever while reading as though the calendar were covered. .current and .dlq are NOT added:
# .current is RESET-REBUILDABLE and .dlq is diagnostics. The raw-only rule stands for both.
# (.events was refused here too, as "outside THIS §40 archival exception". That sentence was about §40.8's
# change distribution, which .events is genuinely no input to, and it is superseded by the FOURTH TOPIC
# paragraph below - not withdrawn as wrong, narrowed to what it was actually about. What that sentence got
# RIGHT and this file keeps: .events is not off every critical path - ArtifactBinding recovers identity and
# level from it, a missing or inconsistent finalisation history gives LEVEL_STATE_LOST, and an unreadable one
# can halt startup. That was never an argument against archiving it.)
#
# THIRD TOPIC, 2026-09-20, same exception widened ONCE and for one reason: options.spx.vol-premium.warnings.
# The sentence above used to say .warnings was "not on the critical path this exception was opened for", which
# was true of the §40.8 change distribution and is NOT true of §40.15 forward evaluation. VP-368 requires every
# early-warning episode to be RETAINED and evaluated forward, and §40.10 makes the episode's terminal
# resolution record the ONLY admissible source of its outcome, close, duration and time to normalisation -
# ForwardEvaluator READS them and refuses to infer them (evaluation/ForwardEvaluator.java; an episode with no
# resolution record is UNRESOLVED and is excluded from every cell). Those records exist nowhere but this topic:
# .ivrv's WarningSummary carries the type, current state, strength, open episode id and opening ordinal, and NO
# terminal outcome or duration - a closed episode's id simply leaves the summary - and .events carries frames
# and session finalisations, not the terminal warning records.
# Nor can a raw-feed replay stand in for them. The point is NOT that a replay would produce a different episode:
# episode identity is deterministic in symbol, session, type and opening frame. It is that a replay establishes
# what an engine WOULD publish today, never what the historical engine DID publish - the episode that was
# evaluated ran against a particular engine build, parameter set and baseline binding, and §40.10 asks what
# happened, not what would happen. So this is the same argument as .ivrv's, applied to the other ledger §40 reads.
#
# MEASURED on the prod broker 2026-09-20, read-only: kafka-get-offsets gave earliest 0 and exclusive latest 63;
# a partition-assigned console read at --isolation-level read_committed with auto-commit disabled (an EARLIER
# count of 35 was wrong twice - it used a group-subscribing read, and Kafka 4.3.0's deprecated --property prints
# a notice to STDOUT, which wc -l counted as a record) found 34 JSON RECORDS: 38,424 B of values plus newlines,
# 3,012 B at gzip level 6. Session dates 2026-09-14..09-18; 17 opening records and 17 terminal records; 16
# NORMALISED outcomes and 1 REVERSED. That is the RETAINED, currently readable history - it is not by itself
# proof of continuity since the shadow deployment - and it excludes archive metadata overhead. The cost is a
# rounding error against .ivrv's ~0.5 MB/session.
#
# CAPTURE - as with the other two, naming it here is the whole change: it is already in
# OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS, and it is correctly NOT in OE_COMMITTED_READ_AS_WRITTEN_TOPICS.
# MIN_RECORDS is 0 DELIBERATELY: a session in which the engine raises no warning at all is a legitimate
# session, and a non-zero floor would turn a quiet day into a nightly alert. (SHADOW with baseline=NONE leaves
# most warning types UNCALIBRATED - that is a missing percentile, not an inability to raise a warning.)
# What zero does NOT give up: the committed reader must still reach its metadata-derived stable boundary; the
# archiver still checks reader status, position, record count and the five-field Offset layout; files and the
# manifest are still published before the checkpoint is stamped; and the verifier still catches missing files,
# manifest corruption, offset discontinuities and checksums.
# What it DOES give up, stated rather than hidden: an ENTIRELY ABSENT .warnings capture verifies as EMPTY and
# passes. There is no cross-topic guard - oe-archive-kafka.sh does not apply MIN_RECORDS at all, and
# oe-archive-verify.sh picks a floor per topic and compares only that topic's own count - so an earlier claim
# here that .ivrv's floor of 400 covers .warnings "since the two are written by the same process" was false and
# is withdrawn. Sharing a producer is not an archive guarantee.
#
# FOURTH TOPIC, 2026-09-20, same exception widened once more and for one reason: the frame ledger
# options.spx.vol-premium.events. NOT a general reopening of the 09-17 rule, and NOT justified by the three
# above it - the precedent they set is that each topic is argued on its own, so this is argued on its own.
# WHAT NEEDS IT: VP-91's drift monitor, VolPremiumGateRunner --mode drift (VOL-PREMIUM-DANGER-CLOCK-GATE1.md
# §38, increment 5, "What the drift job is blocked on"). It scores archived sessions through GateArchiveLoader,
# which reads THIS topic and no other for its frames, and §9's activation criteria are re-scored from nothing
# else. With .events in no archived set every monitored session loads as an empty one and is excluded and
# counted, so the job cannot be built: a cron that alerted on its non-zero exit would be a daily false page.
# WHY NO REPLAY SUBSTITUTES - the .ivrv argument, applied to the ledger §9 and §38 read. It is not that a
# replay would produce different frames. It is that a frame records what the engine DID publish, under the
# baselineVersion, baselineContentHash, calendarVersion, calendarContentHash and codeVersion stamped INTO that
# record (every frame carries all five), and a re-derivation establishes what TODAY's build would publish
# against today's artefacts. A drift monitor whose whole subject is "has the engine moved away from the
# artefacts it was activated under" cannot take its input from a re-derivation of the current engine: the
# quantity it is asked to detect is exactly the one the re-derivation erases. The same topic also carries the
# SESSION_FINALISED chain the level is rebuilt from across sessions (§8.8) and the recovery tail (§8.1(4)).
#
# RETENTION - a correction, so the wrong argument is not made for the right change. .events is NOT a 90-day
# topic and there is no expiry deadline here. §7's table records that it WAS 90 days and now declares delete +
# retention.ms=-1, PROTECTED, RESET-PRESERVED, never recreated; scripts/kafka/topics.env declares the same
# (retention.ms=-1 and retention.bytes=-1), and the prod broker confirms it live today: cleanup.policy=delete,
# retention.ms=-1, retention.bytes=-1, one partition, EARLIEST OFFSET 0 - nothing has expired or ever will.
# So "capture it before retention eats it" is false for this topic and is not the argument. The exposure is the
# one the 09-17 deletion itself demonstrated: a topic no nightly pass takes is one broker or disk loss away
# from being worth nothing, and -1 retention is a promise about the cleaner, not about the disk.
#
# MEASURED on the prod broker 2026-09-20, read-only (kafka-get-offsets for the boundaries, then ONE
# partition-ASSIGNED console read - --partition 0, so no consumer group is joined - at --isolation-level
# read_committed with auto-commit disabled, and --command-property rather than the deprecated --property,
# whose Kafka 4.3.0 notice prints to STDOUT and was counted as a record in #1076's first two attempts; this
# read produced 0 such lines):
#   offsets 0..32,881 exclusive = 32,881 offsets, of which 26,824 are COMMITTED RECORDS (26,818 FRAME and 6
#   SESSION_FINALISED; the remaining 6,057 offsets are transaction markers). 74,195,894 B of values plus
#   newlines over the whole retained log; gzip -6 PER SESSION, which is the shape the archiver actually files:
#     2026-09-11  3,419 records   9,563,091 B -> 141,975 B   (partial: the shadow deployment's first, late-start
#                                                             session - its opening frame is LATE_START_NO_AS_OF_STATE)
#     2026-09-14  4,681 records  12,882,023 B -> 228,702 B
#     2026-09-15  4,681 records  12,909,474 B -> 231,894 B
#     2026-09-16  4,681 records  12,944,888 B -> 223,969 B
#     2026-09-17  4,681 records  12,876,757 B -> 231,991 B
#     2026-09-18  4,681 records  13,019,661 B -> 242,495 B
#   Six sessions, contiguous: 09-12, 09-13 and 09-19 are weekend days, so the log has no gap in it.
# So a FULL session costs ~0.22-0.24 MB of NAS, ~1.2 MB/week, ~0.06 GB/year at 252 sessions - about HALF of
# .ivrv's ~0.5 MB/session, which is the opposite of what "the frame ledger is the biggest of the four" predicts
# and is worth stating rather than assuming: a frame is roughly half the bytes of an ivrv observation, and both
# compress about 53:1. Against ~5 GB/day for the derived backlog the 09-17 rule deleted, and ~257 MB for two
# days of options.databento.raw, the four vol-premium ledgers together are still under ~0.2 GB/year.
#
# CAPTURE - as with the other three, naming it here is the whole change. .events is ALREADY in
# OE_VOL_PREMIUM_TRANSACTIONAL_TOPICS -> OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS (oe-archive-kafka.sh), which it must be:
# vol-premium-service refuses to start without exactly_once_v2, so 6,057 of its 32,881 offsets are markers and
# a plain console consumer would file aborted records as frames the engine never published. It is correctly NOT
# in OE_COMMITTED_READ_AS_WRITTEN_TOPICS, so every capture is proven line by line against the five-field Offset
# layout the loaders admit on. There is no legacy checkpoint to recapture over.
# THE FIRST RUN reads from offset 0 and files the WHOLE retained history in one go - 26,824 records, ~74 MB on
# the broker, ~1.30 MB gzipped, all six sessions - under THAT run's dt=. That is the storage-date rule stated
# further down, not a defect: CommittedLedgerArchive selects a session by the records' own sessionDate, never
# by the folder name, which is exactly how GateArchiveLoader will find 2026-09-14 inside a dt=2026-09-2x file.
OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod="options.ibkr.raw options.databento.raw options.databento.events.raw options.opra.tcbbo options.databento.gex.strike.history options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.state options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.events options.spx.vol-premium.ivrv options.spx.vol-premium.baseline options.spx.vol-premium.warnings options.spx.vol-premium.events"

# The full prod policy = what a complete session looks like. The verifier checks THIS set; the two
# jobs above partition it. A topic that is in neither job but in this set would alert every day,
# which is the correct way to notice that a topic has no producer job.
OE_ALL_TOPICS_prod="$OE_SPOT_TOPICS $OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod"

# ES4 — owned by the 17:01 ET post-close cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=es4) on the prod host, reading the
# es4 broker directly. The cron passes NO TOPICS override (oe-archive.crontab): the archiver
# REQUIRES this variable and derives the set from it. One definition, every caller.
# 2026-08-15 addition: es.futures.cvd.bars only (ES-CVD-DESIGN.md, Gates 1-4).
#   es.futures.cvd.bars — compacted with a UNIQUE key per bar (symbol|timeframe|barStartMs), so
#     compaction is not the threat; es4's clean-reset log wipe is (the 2026-08-11 loss class).
#     This is the CVD history: one record per closed bar per timeframe, all closed by 17:00, so
#     the 17:01 capture holds exactly one whole session.
#   es.futures.cvd (the 1 Hz snapshot) is deliberately NOT here. Measured 2026-08-15 on the
#     first capture attempt: the archiver bounds a read by --max-messages == offset span and
#     relies on a 60s idle timeout to finish early — a compacted topic with a LIVE 1 Hz producer
#     satisfies neither (the span is unreachable through compaction gaps, and it never goes
#     idle), so the consumer starves until the 900s kill (rc=124) and the topic fails EVERY run,
#     forever — a daily false alarm, which is how real alarms die. Its only archival value (the
#     terminal session state) is carried by the session-timeframe bar in cvd.bars anyway.
# 2026-09-07 addition: the ES Footprint history (ES-FOOTPRINT-DESIGN.md F-R14), same loss class.
#   es.futures.footprint.bars — retention.ms=-1, UNIQUE key per closed bar (symbol|timeframe|barStartMs),
#     all closed by 17:00: one whole session per 17:01 capture (reference session: 9,122 records).
#   es.futures.footprint.outcomes — retention.ms=-1, UNIQUE key per F-E6 resolution (the identity); every
#     open item resolves at the session finalize, so the topic is complete and idle by the capture
#     (reference session: 15,339 records).
#   es.futures.footprint and es.futures.footprint.evidence (the change-driven LIVE snapshots with a
#     5 s heartbeat) are deliberately NOT here for exactly the es.futures.cvd reason above: a topic
#     with a producer that never goes idle starves the bounded read to the 900 s kill. Their archival
#     value (the last state of every timeframe) is carried by the closed bars.
#   None of the four is compacted — es4 stamps cleanup.policy=delete on every served topic — so the
#     -1 retention above, not compaction, is what makes bars/outcomes readable as a whole session.
# 2026-09-07 addition: es.futures.auction (SPX Auction Desk, es-amt-service). Plain delete with a
#   LIVE producer, so it was checked against the es.futures.cvd rule above rather than assumed:
#   the key is UNIQUE per minute (`<tradeDate>|<HH:mm>`, one record per RTH minute), nothing is
#   compacted away and the offset span is reachable, unlike the single-key 1 Hz snapshot; and
#   the producer is IDLE after 16:00 ET, so by this job's 17:01 ET run the 60s idle timeout ends the
#   read instead of the 900s kill. Both halves of the starvation hold for cvd and neither holds
#   here. What it needs archiving against is the same thing cvd.bars does: retention is seven
#   calendar days (604800000) and es4's clean-reset wipes the log outright.
# 2026-09-10 addition: es.futures.footprint.strike (ES-FOOTPRINT-STRIKE-INTERACTION.md R6/R13/R14) — the
#   strike-interaction episode log, retention.ms=-1 and retention.bytes=-1, cleanup.policy=delete (every
#   revision survives; the design puts this log in the nightly archive explicitly). A TRANSACTIONAL
#   append-only log: episode keys REPEAT across revisions and CHECKPOINT keys carry no episode at all, so
#   the archive is a log, not a last-value set; and commit/abort markers occupy offsets that never surface
#   as records, so (end - from) overstates the readable count.
#
#   CAPTURE (deploy Codex final review, 2026-09-11). This topic is in OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS
#   (oe-archive-kafka.sh), so kafka-console-consumer never reads it. StrikeArchiveReader.java takes the
#   exclusive boundary from Kafka METADATA — the read_committed end offset, i.e. the last stable offset
#   (LSO), or min(LSO, the UNTIL_TS offset) in bounded mode — writes only records below it, and reports
#   completion only when its consumer position reached it. The checkpoint and the manifest's offset_to
#   are that boundary and nothing else: a transaction still open at capture time holds the boundary
#   BELOW its first record, so those records are captured by the next run once they commit, never
#   skipped; an aborted revision is never archived; a range holding only markers or aborted records
#   completes with zero records and still advances. A reader that times out (STRIKE_READER_DEADLINE_S,
#   default 900 s), fails, or reports anything short of its boundary leaves the checkpoint where it was.
#   Rounds 2-6 recorded why no boundary may be derived from the console consumer's TEXT; this reader
#   never does, and every topic outside OE_COMMITTED_READ_TOPICS (oe-archive-kafka.sh: this log and the
#   vol-premium ledgers) is still captured by the console consumer exactly as before.
#
#   A dt= FOLDER IS A STORAGE DATE, NOT A SESSION (deploy #1041 review). Because a capture stops at the
#   stable boundary, records an open transaction held back at date D's run are archived by the NEXT run,
#   under ITS dt=. Their manifest lines say so: stable_boundary is where the capture stopped, queried_end
#   the end that run meant to reach. Anything reading a SESSION out of these folders must therefore read
#   dt=D and the following storage dates and select by the records' own session identity — and must treat
#   the session as incomplete until committed captures reach that queried_end. That is what
#   options-edge-processing's vol-premium loaders do (calibration/CommittedLedgerArchive), using the
#   per-record Offset column every committed-read file keeps.
#
#   Completion is NOT tied to the producer going idle. The capture ends when the position reaches the
#   stable boundary taken at its start, however busy the log is afterwards; neither the 60 s idle
#   timeout nor the 17:00 finalize is part of this topic's contract. (For every console-consumer topic
#   those mechanisms are unchanged and still prove only what they prove — see the es.futures.cvd note.)
#   The floor below detects grossly missing output; it does not establish session completeness.
#
#   SOURCE PRESERVATION IS CONDITIONAL — retention -1 is NOT a repair guarantee. retention.ms=-1 and
#   retention.bytes=-1 stop RETENTION from deleting records; they do not stop a whole-broker wipe, and
#   es4 has one: scripts/es4/cleanup-es4.sh (Jenkins es4-deploy ACTION=clean-reset) deletes the entire
#   Kafka data directory, after which the same offsets name a different log. A range the archive did not
#   capture can be recaptured from the source ONLY while (1) the topic has not been wiped or re-created
#   since (its TopicId still equals the one in $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.identity) and (2) the
#   range is still inside the retained log (earliest offset <= the range start). Source preservation
#   therefore depends on the archive having COMPLETED before the wipe. The interlock that enforces it:
#   after every durable strike capture the archiver commits its boundary as the offset of consumer group
#   oe-archive-committed-boundary on the es4 broker, and cleanup-es4.sh REFUSES to wipe while that
#   offset is below the strike log end (an absent group counts as nothing archived). The only opt-out is
#   explicit — ES4_STRIKE_ARCHIVE_INTERLOCK=off (Jenkins es4-deploy ACCEPT_UNARCHIVED_STRIKE_LOSS) — and
#   the wipe then logs the loss it was told to accept.
#
#   REPAIR PROCEDURE — a range known or suspected missing from the archive, and ALWAYS before any wipe:
#     1. LOCK. Hold es4 clean-reset (the interlock refuses anyway while the marker is short), and hold
#        the archiver's own topic lock for the whole repair so no scheduled run appends meanwhile:
#        /tmp/oe-archive-kafka.es4.<cksum of ARCHIVE_DIR>.t-es.futures.footprint.strike.lock (flock).
#     2. VERIFY THE SOURCE. kafka-topics --describe must show the TopicId recorded in
#        $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.identity — a different id means the source was re-created and
#        the range is GONE: stop and record the loss. kafka-get-offsets --time earliest must be <= the
#        first offset to recapture, or the head of the range is gone the same way.
#     3. RECAPTURE conservatively, as a SUPERSET of the suspect files. For this topic the archiver resumes
#        ONLY from a checkpoint line it stamped itself ("capture=read_committed_stable_boundary", written
#        after a verified committed-read capture); the LAST line for a partition that is not stamped is
#        LEGACY, and the next run recaptures that partition from the log START (deploy re-review round 2,
#        finding 2). So the repair appends an unstamped line — "0=0 records=0 span=0 dt=<date>
#        archived=<stamp> repair=<ticket>" — to $MAN/es.futures.footprint.strike.offsets and runs the
#        archiver with SESSION_DATE=<date> ARCHIVE_JOB=repair ON_LOCK_BUSY=fail. That run reads the whole
#        retained log (a superset of every suspect range), files it under dt=<date> with
#        "recaptured_over_unproven_checkpoint" in its manifest line, and writes the stamped checkpoint the
#        next scheduled run resumes from. A hand-written offset is never resumed from, so a wrong one
#        cannot skip anything.
#     4. SUPERSEDE the old claims. Move each replaced dt=<date>/*.jsonl.gz and its _manifest.jsonl line
#        into dt=<date>/_superseded/<stamp>/ with a README naming the ticket and the replacement files.
#        The verifier reads only dt=<date>/_manifest.jsonl and the files directly under dt=<date>, so a
#        superseded capture is kept for audit but never counted twice. Then run
#        ENV=es4 oe-archive-verify.sh <date>.
#     5. Release the lock. The archive marker is recorded after the repair's capture, and re-recorded by an
#        idle run only from a stamped checkpoint that reaches the log end; the interlock checks it again
#        before any wipe.
# es.options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.{state,events} added 2026-09-19: the ladder-path forward record. cleanup-es4.sh
# wipes the whole broker and does not read RESET_PRESERVED, so on es4 this nightly capture is its only protection
# (the same level as the other evidence topics here; only footprint.strike carries the wipe interlock). Absent before
# the service first runs on es4 = a confirmed SKIP for the archiver, never a failure.
OE_ES4_TOPICS="es.underlying.es.trades es.tape-zones.cells es.futures.cvd.bars es.futures.footprint.bars es.futures.footprint.outcomes es.futures.footprint.strike es.futures.auction es.options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.state es.options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.events"
# The es4 completeness POLICY — what oe-archive-verify.sh checks for ENV=es4 (it requires
# OE_ALL_TOPICS_<env>). The es4 job owns exactly this set, so the policy is the set itself; without
# this line the es4 floors below (including the strike floor) were declared and never checked.
OE_ALL_TOPICS_es4="$OE_ES4_TOPICS"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPLETENESS FLOORS — records per topic per trading date, below which a date is PARTIAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: "<topic>:<min_records> ..." — anything unlisted defaults to OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT.
#
# These are FLOORS, not expectations: set at roughly a tenth of an observed normal session, so a
# truncated or half-written date trips the alarm while a genuinely quiet session does not. Observed
# counts (2026-08-07, a full session) are in the comments so the next person can see what a normal
# day looks like rather than re-deriving it.
#
# ⚠️ The two feature topics are floored DELIBERATELY LOW — presence checks, not volume checks — and
# both MUST be re-derived from the first clean single-session archive. Measured 2026-08-12 on the
# first-ever capture of each:
#   delta-flow-by-strike            cleanup.policy=delete   → 873,382 records, full history intact
#   option-price-behavior-by-strike cleanup.policy=compact,delete
#                                   → 138,554 readable of 16,564,067 offsets: 0.836% SURVIVED
# OPB's history is destroyed by COMPACTION before any archiver can see it (p0: 61 readable of
# 417,095). A daily run captures mostly the active segment, which the cleaner has not touched yet,
# so its per-session yield is unknown until a full session has run — and guessing a floor now would
# either page every morning or check nothing at all. See ARCHIVE-FAIL-SILENT-FIX-20260812.md §6.
#
# es.futures.auction is one record per RTH minute. A FULL session is 390 minutes (09:30-15:59 ET),
# but an EARLY CLOSE is 210 (09:30-12:59), so the floor is 210: a floor of 390 would page on every
# half day. This floor therefore catches a dead or badly broken archive, NOT a single missing
# minute — and it cannot: the archiver counts RECORDS, while completeness for this topic means 390
# DISTINCT minute keys at their latest revision, and a restart replay re-emits keys the file
# already holds. The exact per-session check belongs to a market-calendar-aware checker that counts
# distinct keys against MarketHours' expected minutes for that date (deploy backlog, PR #994).
#
# A floor of 0 means OPTIONAL: this topic may legitimately produce nothing on a given day, and its
# absence is reported as EMPTY rather than failing the date. Use it only where silence is a real
# state of the world, never to quieten a topic you have not diagnosed — an alarm nobody trusts is
# how the original defect survived four evenings. The dealer-ledger fire topics qualify: verified on
# 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11, prod produced zero signal-fired and zero outcome-scored records on both
# days. Those are days on which no signal met threshold, not days on which archiving failed.
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT="${OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT:-1}"
# gamma-ladder-path: .state heartbeats every 60 s for the whole trade date (>= 390 in RTH alone), so a day under
# 300 is a truncated capture, not a quiet one. .events is data-dependent: 1 = the ANCHOR or NO_FORECAST every
# running day emits.
# vol-premium (2026-09-20): .ivrv measured 4,428 committed records on 2026-09-18, a full session, so 400 is the
# usual tenth-of-a-session floor and still clears an early close (~half a session) without paging. .baseline is
# 0 = OPTIONAL: it gains a record only when a person publishes a baseline, so nearly every day is legitimately
# empty and must be reported EMPTY rather than failing the date.
# .events:400 — and unlike .ivrv's 400 this one is arithmetic, not a convention. The frame cadence is 5 s, so a
# full RTH session is 390*60/5 = 4,680 frames plus the one SESSION_FINALISED record = 4,681, and that is exactly
# what each of the five full sessions measured above holds (4,681, five times over). An early close (09:30-13:00,
# 210 min) is 2,521 by the same arithmetic, so any floor at or below 2,521 clears a half day without paging.
# 400 is the tenth-of-a-session convention .ivrv already uses, kept here deliberately rather than inventing a
# second rule: it is ~1/12 of a session and 1/6 of an early close, which catches a dead or badly broken archive
# and cannot catch a single missing frame — and it must not try to, since the archiver counts RECORDS while
# per-session completeness means 4,680 distinct frames.
# WHAT A NON-ZERO FLOOR BUYS, stated because #1076 had to withdraw the opposite claim for .warnings: the floor
# is the ONLY guard against an entirely absent capture, and it works only when it is non-zero. oe-archive-verify.sh
# reports a date with no records as EMPTY (passing) when the floor is 0 and as MISSING (failing, exit 1, alert)
# otherwise. So .warnings at 0 really does pass when nothing was captured, and .events at 400 does not. There is
# still no cross-topic guard — oe-archive-kafka.sh applies no floor at all and the verifier compares each topic
# only against its own count — so .events' floor protects .events and nothing else.
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_RECORDS="\
dealer-ledger-signal-fired:0 \
dealer-ledger-outcome-scored:0 \
dealer-ledger-calibration-state:0 \
dealer-ledger-state:1000 \
dealer-ledger-profile:1000 \
close.direction.signal:1 \
options.databento.events.raw:500000 \
options.databento.display:300000 \
options.databento.gex.strike:400000 \
options.databento.gex.strike.history:150000 \
options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.state:300 \
options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.events:1 \
options.spx.vol-premium.ivrv:400 \
options.spx.vol-premium.baseline:0 \
options.spx.vol-premium.warnings:0 \
options.spx.vol-premium.events:400 \
es.options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.state:300 \
es.options.spx.gamma-ladder-path.events:1 \
delta-flow-by-strike:1000 \
option-price-behavior-by-strike:100 \
underlying.spx.index.price:5000 \
underlying.es.price:20000 \
underlying.spx.price:1000 \
spx.basis.state:100 \
underlying.vix.index.price:50 \
es.futures.cvd.bars:500 \
es.futures.footprint.bars:500 \
es.futures.footprint.outcomes:500 \
# strike provisionally shares the source-bars floor: every well-formed bar that advances the watermark leaves
# episode records or a CHECKPOINT, so a day with 500 bars leaves at least 500 records. A coarse presence floor,
# not proof of completeness — re-derive from the first complete archive, including an early close.
es.futures.footprint.strike:500 \
es.futures.auction:210 \
underlying.vix.price:100"
